WEBVTT

0
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:06.440
We know that optical circuit switching

1
00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:09.439
redirects light without electrical conversion.

2
00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:12.119
But how exactly does it do that?

3
00:00:13.109 --> 00:00:15.810
Let's look inside two implementations,

4
00:00:16.149 --> 00:00:16.709
MEMS

5
00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:18.610
and silicon photonic,

6
00:00:18.739 --> 00:00:22.110
and compare how each technology redirects light.

7
00:00:23.239 --> 00:00:26.639
Let us start with micro electromechanical systems,

8
00:00:26.930 --> 00:00:27.639
or MEMS.

9
00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:31.420
This is mature beam steering technology,

10
00:00:31.719 --> 00:00:34.479
powering hyperscalar OCS deployments.

11
00:00:35.549 --> 00:00:38.990
Including Google's Palomar Optical Interconnect

12
00:00:39.189 --> 00:00:41.650
for TPUV4 supercomputers.

13
00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:45.119
Inside a MEMS optical switch,

14
00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:48.880
you will not find silicon logic gates routing packets.

15
00:00:49.009 --> 00:00:49.610
Instead,

16
00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:52.590
you will find intricate two-dimensional

17
00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:57.130
or three-dimensional arrays of microscopic tiltable mirrors.

18
00:00:57.950 --> 00:00:57.959
When

19
00:00:58.345 --> 00:01:02.284
Aimated or parallel light beams enter the switch from an input fiber.

20
00:01:02.525 --> 00:01:04.745
It hits one of these tiny mirrors.

21
00:01:04.964 --> 00:01:06.504
A voltage is applied,

22
00:01:06.764 --> 00:01:10.084
physically tilting the mirror to steer the beam through

23
00:01:10.084 --> 00:01:13.385
free space directly into the target output port.

24
00:01:14.739 --> 00:01:17.879
This physical steering happens in milliseconds

25
00:01:18.019 --> 00:01:22.239
and supports very large port counts with low optical loss.

26
00:01:23.389 --> 00:01:26.629
But because it relies on precise physical alignment,

27
00:01:26.959 --> 00:01:27.980
these designs

28
00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:29.660
can be sensitive to vibration.

29
00:01:30.839 --> 00:01:33.269
Now let us look at silicon photonics.

30
00:01:33.529 --> 00:01:37.580
This technology takes a completely solid state approach.

31
00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:42.120
It routes photons through submicron on chip waveguides,

32
00:01:42.319 --> 00:01:46.099
similar to how traditional integrated circuits route electrons.

33
00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:50.260
Inside a silicon photonic switch,

34
00:01:50.419 --> 00:01:52.199
there are no moving mirrors.

35
00:01:52.459 --> 00:01:53.139
Instead,

36
00:01:53.379 --> 00:01:59.010
the routing fabric is built from components such as mxander interferometers,

37
00:01:59.330 --> 00:02:00.209
couplers,

38
00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:01.809
ring resonators,

39
00:02:02.099 --> 00:02:03.680
and phase shifters.

40
00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:06.150
To redirect the optical path,

41
00:02:06.419 --> 00:02:08.800
the switch applies a thermo optic

42
00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:11.199
or electro-optic actuator

43
00:02:11.419 --> 00:02:14.240
that changes the waveguide's refractive index.

44
00:02:15.179 --> 00:02:17.639
That creates a controlled phase shift

45
00:02:17.899 --> 00:02:20.130
in a Moxzander interferometer.

46
00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:23.639
The resulting interference directs optical power

47
00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:26.279
toward one output path or the other.

48
00:02:26.899 --> 00:02:29.119
Speed depends on the actuator.

49
00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:33.660
Electro-optic designs can switch on a nanosecond scale,

50
00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:36.529
while thermo optic designs are slower,

51
00:02:36.770 --> 00:02:37.199
often

52
00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:39.279
in the microsecond range.

53
00:02:40.399 --> 00:02:41.649
As fabrics grow,

54
00:02:41.770 --> 00:02:43.789
insertion loss and crosstal

55
00:02:44.169 --> 00:02:45.729
become important limits.

56
00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:47.240
In some designs,

57
00:02:47.490 --> 00:02:52.000
semiconductor optical amplifiers can compensate for loss,

58
00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:52.710
although

59
00:02:53.089 --> 00:02:54.149
they add power

60
00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:56.050
and integration complexity.

61
00:02:58.009 --> 00:03:01.240
Both technologies offer incredible efficiency

62
00:03:01.419 --> 00:03:06.520
but serve different architectural needs based on your required switching speed

63
00:03:06.740 --> 00:03:07.839
and port counts.